course: everyday
lesson: 61
title: Cats and dogs up close
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cats and dogs cannot talk, but they tell us how they feel with their tails, ears, faces and bodies, and a good friend to animals learns to read these signals.
explain: A cat that lashes or thumps its tail while you stroke it is saying «enough»; a dog that yawns, licks its lips and turns its head away is saying «I feel uneasy». In both cases you stop and give the animal space.
explain: Many things people do out of love feel frightening to animals: a hug around a dog's neck feels like a trap, and a face pushed close to a dog's face is how many bites to children happen.
explain: Some old beliefs are wrong and can hurt pets: cats do not always land safely on their feet, most grown cats get tummy upsets from cow's milk, and cooked bones are dangerous for dogs.
explain: A girl in Colorado meets a strange cat on the sidewalk. She holds a finger out low and waits; the cat sniffs it and rubs its cheek on her hand, and only then does she stroke it gently.
explain: A boy in Ohio tried to take a bone away from his dog while it was eating; the dog snapped and bit his hand, and he needed stitches.
explain: A family in Leeds kept lilies in a vase where their cat could reach; the cat chewed a leaf, its kidneys began to fail and it needed days of treatment at the vet.
fact: A cat that swishes, lashes or thumps its tail while being stroked is annoyed and may soon scratch or bite, so you stop and let it walk away.
fact: A cat that rubs against your legs is leaving its scent on you, a friendly way of saying you belong to it.
fact: You let a strange cat sniff a finger held low and stroke it only if it rubs against you.
fact: Most adult cats cannot digest the sugar in milk well, so cats should drink fresh water, not cow's milk.
fact: Cats can be badly hurt in falls, so you never drop or throw a cat and keep windows and balconies safe.
fact: A kitten should play with a wand toy, not your fingers, or it learns that hands are toys and bites harder when grown.
fact: A cat that stops eating, or keeps going to the litter box without passing anything, may be seriously ill and needs a vet quickly.
fact: The litter box is scooped every day and hands are washed after, because cat droppings can carry germs such as toxoplasma; pregnant women should not clean it.
fact: Most dogs feel trapped when hugged, so you stroke a dog's chest or side and keep your face away from its face.
fact: Yawning, lip licking and turning the head away are calm-down signals that show a dog feels uneasy.
fact: A guide dog or assistance dog in a harness is working, so you do not stroke, call or feed it.
fact: In some places a yellow ribbon or yellow lead on a dog means «this dog needs space».
fact: Cooked bones splinter and can choke a dog or hurt its insides.
fact: If a pavement is too hot for the back of your hand after a few seconds, it can burn a dog's paws.
fact: Many dog bites happen when someone touches a dog while it is eating, sleeping or chewing a toy.
fact: If a dog has something in its mouth it should not, you never grab it out; you swap it for a treat or get an adult.
fact: Lilies are very poisonous to cats, and even a small amount can make them seriously ill.
exercise: You are stroking a cat and its tail starts to lash and thump. What should you do? | Stop stroking and let the cat walk away | Stroke it faster and harder to calm it down | Hold the tail still so it relaxes | Pick the cat up and hug it
exercise: How often should a cat's litter box be scooped? | Every day | Once a week | Once a month | Only when it starts to smell
exercise: Why should a pregnant woman not clean the cat's litter box? | Cat droppings can carry germs such as toxoplasma | The full litter tray is far too heavy for her to lift | Cats do not like pregnant women | The scoop is too sharp to hold
exercise: What is the best drink to give a pet cat? | Fresh water | A saucer of cow's milk | Cold sweet tea | A glass of fruit juice
exercise: Why does cow's milk often upset a grown cat's tummy? | Most adult cats cannot digest the sugar in milk well | Milk is too cold for cats | Milk has much too much salt in it for a cat's body | Cats only drink milk from goats
exercise: A cat you do not know walks up to you outside. What is the polite and safe thing to do? | Hold a finger out low and let it sniff | Grab it quickly before it runs away | Stroke its tail and back legs first | Pick it up to see if it has a collar
exercise: Is it true that a cat always lands safely on its feet? | No, cats can be badly hurt in falls | Yes, so dropping a cat cannot hurt it | Yes, but only from a balcony | No, cats always land on their backs
exercise: A kitten loves to bite and scratch your hands when you play. What should you play with instead? | A wand toy that keeps your fingers away | Your bare hands, until it learns to be gentle | Your toes under the blanket | Nothing, kittens should not play
exercise: A cat keeps going to the litter box but passes nothing. What should you do? | Tell an adult straight away | Wait a week to see if it stops | Give it a saucer of cold milk | Move the box to another room
exercise: Why does a cat rub against your legs? | It leaves its scent to say you belong to it | It wants to trip you over | It is trying to clean its dusty fur on your trousers | It is warning you to go away
exercise: How should you show love to a friendly dog? | Stroke its chest or side gently | Hug it tightly around the neck | Put your face right up to its face | Pull it onto your lap
exercise: A dog you are playing with yawns, licks its lips and turns its head away. What does this mean? | It feels uneasy and needs some space | It is tired and wants a long cuddle on your lap | It is hungry and wants a treat | It wants you to play rougher
exercise: A dog has a yellow ribbon tied to its lead. What should you do? | Not go near the dog | Ask to stroke it straight away | Throw it a treat to make friends | Untie the ribbon for the owner
exercise: You see a guide dog in a harness in a shop. What should you do? | Leave it alone so it can work | Call its name so it comes to you | Give it a dog biscuit as a reward | Stroke it while its owner is busy
exercise: Your dog finds the cooked chicken bones from dinner. What is true? | Cooked bones splinter and can hurt a dog | Cooked bones are the best treat for dogs | Cooked bones are only bad for cats | Cooked bones keep a dog's teeth clean
exercise: How can you check if the pavement is too hot for a dog's paws? | Hold the back of your hand on it for a few seconds | Look whether the sun is shining | Ask the dog to walk on it first and see if it minds | Touch it quickly with one fingertip
exercise: Why are many children's dog bites to the face? | Children often put their faces close to a dog's face | Dogs are taught to bite faces | Dogs cannot see children's hands | Children's faces smell like food to dogs, so they bite them
test: A boy in Ohio says his cat lands on its feet, so it is fine to let it sit on an open fifth-floor balcony. Which is right? | No, cats can be badly hurt falling from balconies | Yes, cats are never hurt by falls, they always land safely | Yes, as long as the cat is young | No, but only because of the wind
test: A girl in Brisbane is stroking her cat on her lap when its tail begins to thump on her leg. What will probably happen if she keeps going? | The cat may scratch or bite her | The cat will fall asleep | The cat will start to purr more | Nothing, tail thumping means it is happy
test: A boy in Glasgow is visiting his gran, whose cat has not eaten for two days. What is best? | Tell his gran, because the cat may need a vet | Leave a saucer of milk out to tempt the cat to eat | Wait until the cat gets hungry enough | Give the cat some of his sweets
test: Which of these is a sign that a dog feels uncomfortable? | It licks its lips and looks away | It wags its whole body and bounces | It brings you its ball to throw for it | It rolls over on its back to play
test: A man with a white cane walks past with a dog in a harness. A girl in Toronto wants to say hello to the dog. What should she do? | Not distract the dog, because it is working | Whistle so the dog turns to her | Offer it a piece of her sandwich as a friendly hello | Pat its head quickly as it passes
test: It is a sunny July afternoon in Phoenix and the sidewalk feels burning to the back of your hand. What does this mean for a dog walk? | It could burn the dog's paws, so wait or use the grass | Dogs' paws never feel heat | A walk is fine if the dog walks fast | Only puppies can be burned by pavements
test: Why is it better to play with a kitten using a toy on a string than with your fingers? | It learns that hands are not for biting | Fingers are too slow for kittens to chase | Toys make kittens grow faster | Kittens cannot see fingers well
test: A cat you do not know comes over and rubs its cheek against your hand. What does that tell you? | It is friendly and you can stroke it gently | It wants you to leave at once | It is about to bite you | It is asking to be picked up and carried around the garden
test: Why is a hug around the neck a bad idea even with a gentle family dog? | Most dogs feel trapped and some may bite | Dogs do not feel anything when they are hugged | It makes the dog's fur fall out | Dogs only like hugs from adults
test: kitty's tail going thump thump while i pet her, she likes it? | No, she is annoyed, stop and let her go | Yes, thumping means she is very happy | Yes, it means she wants more strokes | It means she is hungry for food
test: can i give the cat some milk, she looks thirsty | Give her fresh water, milk can upset her tummy | Yes, milk is the best drink there is for cats | Yes, but only warm milk | Only give her milk with sugar
test: dog's in a vest, its a guide dog. can i pat him real quick | No, he is working, so do not stroke him | Yes, a quick pat is always fine | Yes, if you give him a treat too, he'll love it | Only if you call his name first
test: pavement's too hot for my hand, still ok to walk the dog? | No, it can burn his paws, wait till cooler | Yes, dogs' paws never get burned | Yes, if you walk him faster | Yes, if he has had a drink
test: dog's chewing my sock, i'll just grab it outta his mouth | Get an adult or swap it for a treat instead | Pull it out fast | Hold his mouth shut | Shout at him until he gets scared and drops it
test: mum got lilies, cat keeps sniffing em | Tell mum to move them, lilies poison cats | It is fine, cats know what not to eat | Let the cat sniff, just not lick | Put the vase higher on the table
test: Sam says sleeping dogs love being woken with a hug. true? | No, a startled dog may bite | Yes, dogs love a good surprise | Yes, it is the best time to hug | Yes, unless it snores loudly
test: heard a cat scratched a kid's face cos he kept stroking it while its tail was going mad | Real lesson: a thumping tail means an annoyed cat, so stop | Real lesson: cats scratch for no reason | It proves cats only scratch boys | It is gossip, so tails mean nothing
test: my nan says give the kitten a saucer of milk, cats love milk | Most adult cats cannot digest milk well, so fresh water is better | She is right, milk is their best drink | She is right, if the milk is cold | She is right, cats need milk daily
test: the Browns' cat fell off their balcony and people say they don't care about it, pass it on | Do not pass it on; you do not know them, and the lesson is to keep balconies safe | Pass it on, since the fall proves it | Pass it on, so they get another cat | Pass it on, and say it fell twice and they just laughed about it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a cat rubbing against a person's legs? | The cat is leaving its scent upon the person in a friendly manner | The cat is attempting to trip the person | The cat is warning the person to depart | The cat is cleaning its fur upon the person's clothing
exam: Identify the toy with which a kitten ought to play. | A wand toy rather than a person's fingers | A person's bare fingers | A loose piece of string left unattended | A small plastic bag
exam: It being given that a cat repeatedly visits the litter box without passing anything, select the appropriate action. | To consult a veterinary surgeon promptly, as it may be seriously ill | To wait for several days to observe whether matters improve of their own accord | To change the litter and ignore it | To give the cat a large meal
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning the hugging of dogs. | Most dogs feel trapped when hugged, so the chest or side is stroked instead | All dogs enjoy hugs from children | Dogs should be hugged tightly around the neck so that they feel safe and secure | Dogs prefer hugs from strangers
